SRC/sol2 -- sol and solx, as built for OS-9/68000=================================================ORIG holds the post as comp.sources.games carried it.  sol.c and solx.cdiffer from it in three ways:  - their help and rules text was printed with strings continued over many    lines.  Microware's cpp counts a line after joining the continuations,    even inside a skipped #if, and stops with a bus error past 512    characters, so each long string is printed a line at a time here.  - SIGTERM, which OS-9's signal.h lacks, is defined as 15 under OSK.  - Microware's termlib keeps the pad character as PC_ and BC and UP as    pointers; the programs wrote them as a char and two-character arrays,    which would overwrite termlib's pointers.  Under OSK getterm() points BC    and UP at buffers of its own, and a terminal with no pc capability no    longer reads through a null pointer.sys/ioctl.h and solio.c are new: the TIOCGETP and TIOCSETN calls the programsmake, mapped onto _gs_opt() and _ss_opt().  While a game runs the terminal'sinterrupt, abort, end-of-file and pause keys are cleared, and rawmode(FALSE)puts every setting back.